{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the serpent.</b> Some say the woman understood and knew the language of animals, and they interpret \"and the serpent said\" as by allusion. Others said that it is Satan, but how do they not see the end of the passage, for \"upon your belly you shall go,\" and how would he eat dust, and what is the meaning of the curse, \"He will bruise your head\"? And many have gone astray investigating why the serpent was cursed, and whether it had full intelligence, or whether He commanded it not so that it would lead the woman astray. And Rav Saadia Gaon said: since it has been established for us that there is no speech and intelligence except in man alone, we are compelled to say that the serpent and also the donkey did not speak; rather, an angel spoke on their behalf. And Rav Shmuel ben Hofni refuted him, and behold, R. Shlomo the Sefardi rose — the master of metered poems — and he was a great sage, and he refuted Rav Shmuel. And what seems right to me is that the words are as they sound: the serpent spoke, and it walked upright, and the One who placed intelligence in man placed it in it as well. And behold, the verse testified that it was \"more cunning than every beast of the field\" — but not like man. And the meaning of ערום is wise, one who does his deeds with cunning. And do not marvel on account of ערום being after ערומים, for these are two meanings; it is like these wordplays in language, as in \"with the jawbone of a donkey, a heap, two heaps\" (Judges 15:16), and similarly \"thirty towns, and thirty towns for them\" (ibid. 10:4). Moreover, if an angel spoke on its mouth, there is no sin for the serpent, and this angel cannot be the emissary of HaShem, for an angel does not disobey the word of HaShem. And those who ask how (Genesis 3 — if thirteen) the serpent found the woman — this is no question." "And the meaning of אף כי is that it implies that he spoke other things beforehand. And at the end he said an a fortiori argument: He said to you, you shall not eat the fruit of any tree whatsoever. \"The serpent did not mention the honored and awesome Name, for they did not know it. Also the woman added to the commandment of HaShem, \"and you shall not touch it,\" and so Manoah's wife added, \"until the day of his death\" (Judges 13:7)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The parenthetical '(וי\"ג אנה)' is obscure; it appears to reference Genesis 3 by standard abbreviation (וי\"ג = וירא/ויהי ג = ch. 3) with an unclear second word, possibly 'אנה' ('where'), thus 'where in Genesis 3' — translated hyper-literally. The phrase 'השָם דעת' read as 'placed [שום] intelligence,' but the vocalization in the source (with a kamatz under the shin) may reflect a variant or scribal form.", 1 "The quotation marks around the latter portion are retained as in the source; the boundary of the quoted or highlighted material in the Hebrew is not fully certain."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:49.519345904Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}